0,Guiqiong language - Wikipedia,https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guiqiong_language,"Guiqiong (autonym: ɡuʨhiɐŋ; simplified Chinese: 贵琼; traditional Chinese: 貴瓊; pinyin: Guìqióng) is a Qiangic language of Sichuan, China. [2] There are differences in the phonology of the dialects, but communication is possible.",Guiqiong,Sino-Tibetan
1,Chinese Gu Qui traditional Music,https://music.youtube.com/playlist?list=PLNKcOByTrFjsyUbpELZSo06znkgm1zNvU,"Listen to Chinese Gu Qui traditional Music on YouTube Music - a dedicated music app with official songs, music videos, remixes, covers, and more.",Guiqiong,Sino-Tibetan
2,Top Ten Famous Ancient Chinese Guqin Music Pieces,https://guqinworld.com/top-ten-famous-ancient-chinese-guqin-music-pieces/,"An aspiring politician of the royal courts who had dreams of serving the masses, he was subsequently disillusioned by the corruption and infighting there. Leaving politics, he retreated into the deep woods where he was completely surrounded by nature and spent his days playing music and composing poems, often while intoxicated.",Guiqiong,Sino-Tibetan
3,Best Collection of Chinese Traditional Music: Guqin,https://music.youtube.com/playlist?list=OLAK5uy_k43WPBbRSBaZDBW64I9KmQ00YSbj469xA,"Listen to Best Collection of Chinese Traditional Music: Guqin by Various Artists on YouTube Music - a dedicated music app with official songs, music videos, remixes, covers, and more.",Guiqiong,Sino-Tibetan
4,Guiqiong language — Grokipedia,https://grokipedia.com/page/guiqiong_language,"Guiqiong (autonym: ɡuʨhiɐŋ), also romanized as Guìqióng, is a Qiangic language within the Tibeto-Burman branch of the Sino-Tibetan family, spoken by a small alpine ethnic community primarily in Kangding County, Garzê Tibetan Autonomous Prefecture, Sichuan Province, China. [1] [2] The language features a complex phonology with tonal contrasts and distinctive verbal and nominal morphology ...",Guiqiong,Sino-Tibetan
5,Guiqiong in China people group profile | Joshua Project,https://joshuaproject.net/people_groups/11981/CH,"The Guiqiong have been included as part of the Tibetan nationality in China. As early as 1930, however, Chinese researchers stated, ""The language and customs of the Guiqiong are distinct from those of either the Chinese or Khampa Tibetans. The people here are actually a unique ethnic group.""",Guiqiong,Sino-Tibetan
6,"The traditional music of the Qiang, Pumi and Lisu ... - 資訊咖",https://inf.news/en/culture/f06f61414c45d182ef5689fbc0d80da1.html,"The Sino-Tibetan language family is one of the largest and most dynamic language families in the world, with more than one billion speakers. What I want to introduce today is the traditional music of the Qiang, Pumi and Lisu people under the Sino-Tibetan language family.",Guiqiong,Sino-Tibetan
7,Listen Guqin Music Online - chineseculture.net,https://www.chineseculture.net/guqin/qinmusic.htm,"In Imperial China, a well educated scholar was expected to be skilled in four arts: Qin (the guqin), Qi (the game of Go), Shu (calligraphy) and Hua (painting). Historically, the guqin has been viewed as a symbol of Chinese high culture, but today only less than two thousands people can play it, and it is rarely seen in China. Listen Guqin music ...",Guiqiong,Sino-Tibetan
8,"Min, Yang, & Gates: Relativization in Guiqiong",https://www.jbe-platform.com/docserver/fulltext/ltba.18002.min.pdf?expires=1703511837&id=id&accname=id22136091&checksum=8194DD24B86FB93F5BB344CCA5D95327,"Guiqiong, like most Sino-Tibetan languages, presents a rich array of rela-tivization constructions. Based on both natural oral texts and elicited mater-ial, the present paper describes all attested types of relatives in Guiqiong, including prenominal, head-internal, headless, and double-headed relative clauses, as well as nominalized and non-nominalized relative clauses. It pro-vides a case by ...",Guiqiong,Sino-Tibetan
